      "output": "**Water (H₂O)** is a simple yet remarkably versatile chemical compound that is essential for life and plays a central role in Earth’s physical and biological systems. Here’s a concise overview:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical Formula** | H₂O – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular Structure** | Bent shape (≈104.5° bond angle) giving it a polar character. |\n| **States of Matter** | Solid (ice), liquid (water), gas (vapor). Transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing) and 100 °C (boiling) under standard atmospheric pressure. |\n| **Key Physical Properties** | • **High specific heat** – absorbs a lot of heat before temperature rises, moderating climates. <br>• **High surface tension** – allows insects to walk on it and creates droplets. <br>• **Excellent solvent** – dissolves many substances, enabling biochemical reactions. <br>• **Transparency** – allows light penetration, supporting aquatic life. |\n| **Biological Importance** | • Constitutes ~70 % of the human body and most other organisms. <br>• Medium for metabolic reactions, nutrient transport, and waste removal. <br>• Regulates temperature through sweating and respiration. |\n| **Environmental Role** | • Drives the hydrologic cycle (evaporation, condensation, precipitation). <br>• Shapes landscapes via erosion and weathering. <br>• Supports ecosystems from oceans to rainforests. |\n| **Unique Features** | • **Anomalous expansion** – water expands when it freezes, making ice less dense than liquid water, which keeps bodies of water from freezing solid from the bottom up. <br>• **High cohesion and adhesion** – enables capillary action, allowing plants to transport water from roots to leaves. |\n\nIn short, water is a polar, tri‑atomic molecule that exists naturally in three states, possesses extraordinary physical properties, and is indispensable for life, climate regulation, and geological processes."
